# Why Your PHP POST Form Throws "Undefined Array Key" Errors

If you are replicating a standard tutorial example and encountering `Warning: Undefined array key` errors when using `$_POST`, but seeing everything work fine with `$_GET`, the issue isn't usually your server configuration. It is almost certainly a mismatch between how your form is sending data and how your script is receiving it.

In the specific scenario described, you have an HTML form configured with `method="post"`:

```html
<form action="welcome.php" method="post">
    Name: <input type="text" name="name"><br>
    E-mail: <input type="text" name="email"><br>
    <input type="submit">
</form>
```

However, your `welcome.php` file attempts to access `$_POST["name"]` and `$_POST["email"]`. If the server logs show an "Undefined array key" warning, it means the `$_POST` superglobal is empty. This happens because the browser actually sent a GET request, not a POST request.

This often occurs due to one of two reasons: either the form submission was triggered by a button or link that defaults to GET, or there is a JavaScript redirect interfering before the data reaches your PHP script. If you replaced `$_POST` with `$_REQUEST` and it started working, you are masking the symptom rather than solving the root cause. While `$_REQUEST` merges `$_GET`, `$_POST`, and `$_COOKIE`, relying on it makes your application less secure and harder to debug. You want to explicitly handle POST data to ensure high scores are saved correctly via the HTTP protocol intended for state-changing operations.

To fix this, you must ensure the form submission method strictly matches the variable access. If the form tag says `method="post"`, the input element triggering the action should be a `<button type="submit">` or an `<input type="submit">`. Avoid using links (`<a href="welcome.php">`) to trigger the form submission, as browsers default anchor tags to GET requests. Additionally, check for any JavaScript in your application that might be intercepting the form submit event and redirecting via `window.location` without sending a POST request.

If you are building this on top of a framework like Laravel, the approach changes slightly but remains focused on strict separation of concerns. In a standard PHP file as shown in the W3Schools example, ensure no external scripts or server-side redirects are altering the request method before `welcome.php` executes. You can verify the actual HTTP verb your server received by logging it at the very top of your script:

```php
<?php
http_response_code(200); // Optional: Ensure success code is sent if needed
echo "Received Method: " . $_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] . "<br>";

if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
    echo "Processing POST data...<br>";
    // Safe access to post data
    $name = $_POST['name'] ?? '';
} else {
    echo "Received a GET request. Form likely not submitted correctly.";
}
?>
```

By explicitly checking `$_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD']`, you gain visibility into whether the browser is actually sending a POST or if something is redirecting it to GET before your logic runs. This is crucial for applications saving high scores or processing payments, where accidental GET requests can lead to infinite loops in forms or security vulnerabilities known as "Open Redirect" attacks.

Finally, remember that `$_POST` data is never sent in the URL bar. If you see input values appearing in the address bar after submission, your form is definitely not using POST. Stick to `<form method="post">`, ensure submit buttons are used instead of links, and avoid `$_REQUEST` unless you have a specific reason to merge data sources, as it obscures the distinction between safe GET parameters and sensitive POST payloads.
